            <ArticleTitle>'Pseudotumour' invading the proximal femur with normal metal ions following metal on metal hip resurfacing.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2014-206368</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr2014206368</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 75-year-old woman who had undergone hybrid metal-on-metal hip resurfacing 8 years earlier underwent revision arthroplasty because of hip, groin and lateral thigh pain. The main differential was aseptic loosening; however, serum cobalt and chromium levels were normal. Multiple imaging modalities revealed a periprosthetic, cystic soft tissue mass adjacent to the proximal femur. A large 'pseudotumour' with proximal femoral invasion was found at revision arthroplasty. We report the first finding of a 'pseudotumour' invading the proximal femur with normal metal ions following metal on metal hip resurfacing.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>2015 BMJ Publishing Group Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
